## Why This Is Asked
This question tests your ability to design a fixed-memory streaming algorithm for heavy hitter detection—a common requirement in real-world scenarios like analytics dashboards, network monitoring, and recommendation systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Misra-Gries algorithm**: A space-efficient algorithm for finding items that appear more than 1/m of the time
- **Heavy hitters**: Values that appear frequently in a stream (above a threshold)
- **Space-time tradeoffs**: Analyzing memory usage versus per-element update time

## Algorithm Analysis
| Metric | Complexity |
|--------|------------|
| Per-element update | O(1) |
| Per-batch query | O(m) = O(1) since m=2 |
| Memory usage | O(m) = O(2) = O(1) fixed |

## Code Example
```javascript
class MisraGries {
  constructor(m = 2) {
    this.m = m;
    this.counters = new Map();
  }

  add(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x) + 1);
    } else if (this.counters.size < this.m) {
      this.counters.set(x, 1);
    } else {
      for (const [key, val] of this.counters) {
        this.counters.set(key, val - 1);
        if (this.counters.get(key) === 0) this.counters.delete(key);
      }
    }
  }

  getHeavyHitters() {
    return Array.from(this.counters.entries())
      .filter(([_, count]) => count > 0)
      .map(([value, count]) => ({ value, count }));
  }
}
```

## Small Example (m=2, stream: 1, 2, 1, 3, 1, 2)
| Element | Counter State | Heavy Hitters |
|---------|---------------|---------------|
| 1 | {1:1} | 1 |
| 2 | {1:1, 2:1} | 1, 2 |
| 1 | {1:2, 2:1} | 1, 2 |
| 3 | {1:1, 2:0} → {1:1} | 1 |
| 1 | {1:2} | 1 |
| 2 | {1:2, 2:1} | 1, 2 |

## Follow-up Questions
- **Sliding windows**: Use a timestamped variant or combine with a separate structure tracking element ages
- **Ties**: Resolve by recency (most recently incremented) or arbitrarily since counts are equal
- **Accuracy**: Misra-Gries guarantees all heavy hitters are found but may include false positives; lowering the threshold (increasing m) reduces false positives at higher memory cost

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of dynamic connectivity under incremental edge insertions and practical amortized analysis in graph algorithms.

## Key Concepts

- Disjoint Set Union (DSU) with path compression and union by size
- Amortized α(n) (inverse Ackermann function) for near-constant operations
- Batch processing efficiency vs full recomputation approaches

## Code Example

```javascript
class DSU {
  constructor(n) {
    this.parent = Array.from({length: n}, (_, i) => i);
    this.size = new Array(n).fill(1);
    this.components = n;
  }

  find(a) {
    if (this.parent[a] !== a) {
      this.parent[a] = this.find(this.parent[a]);
    }
    return this.parent[a];
  }

  union(a, b) {
    const rootA = this.find(a);
    const rootB = this.find(b);
    if (rootA === rootB) return false;

    if (this.size[rootA] < this.size[rootB]) {
      [rootA, rootB] = [rootB, rootA];
    }
    this.parent[rootB] = rootA;
    this.size[rootA] += this.size[rootB];
    this.components--;
    return true;
  }
}

function processBatches(n, batches) {
  const dsu = new DSU(n);
  const results = [];

  for (const batch of batches) {
    for (const [u, v] of batch) {
      dsu.union(u, v);
    }
    results.push(dsu.components);
  }

  return results;
}
```

## Analysis

**Preprocessing:** O(n) time to initialize DSU arrays, O(n) memory for parent and size arrays.

**Per-batch updates:** O(B·α(n)) time for B union operations with path compression and union by size.

**Total complexity:** O(n + m·α(n)) time, O(n) memory.

**Comparison to recomputation:** Full DFS/BFS after each batch would cost O(n + m) per batch, totaling O((n + m)·(m/B)) time—significantly worse for large graphs with many small batches.

The DSU approach leverages amortized near-constant operations, making it ideal for dynamic connectivity scenarios with incremental updates.

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical implementation of order-statistics data structures with batched updates. It expects a solution that balances fast batch updates with O(1) median queries by maintaining the median explicitly.

## Key Concepts
- Two balanced structures (lower and upper halves) to partition the data
- Lazy/deferred deletions: mark elements as deleted rather than removing them immediately, deferring cleanup to future operations
- Amortized analysis for batch updates and overall complexity
- Trade-offs between simple heaps and BST-based structures

## Code Example
```python
import heapq

class MedianTracker:
    def __init__(self):
        self.low = []   # max-heap (negatives)
        self.high = []  # min-heap
        self.deleted = set()

    def _cleanup(self):
        while self.low and -self.low[0] in self.deleted:
            self.deleted.remove(-heapq.heappop(self.low))
        while self.high and self.high[0] in self.deleted:
            self.deleted.remove(heapq.heappop(self.high))

    def insert(self, x):
        if not self.low or x <= -self.low[0]:
            heapq.heappush(self.low, -x)
        else:
            heapq.heappush(self.high, x)
        self._rebalance()
        self._cleanup()

    def delete(self, x):
        self.deleted.add(x)
        self._cleanup()

    def _rebalance(self):
        if len(self.low) < len(self.high):
            heapq.heappush(self.low, -heapq.heappop(self.high))
        elif len(self.low) - len(self.high) > 1:
            heapq.heappush(self.high, -heapq.heappop(self.low))

    def median_at_least(self, T):
        self._cleanup()
        return -self.low[0] >= T if self.low else False
```

